# Pipeline self-modification guard (F1-S7, issue #10; #160; AGENTS.md
# "Pipeline self-modification"; factory.md §13 point 3).
#
# Mirrors PROTECTED_PATH_PREFIXES and PROTECTED_EXACT_PATHS in
# scripts/factory/implement-patch-logic.mts's findForbiddenPatchPaths — that
# function already rejects any implementing agent's patch that touches these
# paths at all (a fail-closed publish-time guard, unit tested); this file adds
# the complementary human-review control at the GitHub level (required
# code-owner review, once branch protection's require_code_owner_reviews is
# enabled — it is OFF today, so these rules are forward-looking documentation
# and the guard is the sole mechanical control, #160/#163) for the
# conventional, human-directed PRs that legitimately DO touch these paths.
#
# The mirror is now MECHANICALLY ENFORCED, not just requested (#160):
# tests/factory/protected-surface-lockstep.test.ts asserts, byte-for-byte,
# that every exported guard prefix `p` has a `/p` rule here owned @syamaner
# and every exact path has its `/path` rule (direction A), and that every
# rule below points at a guard-protected path (direction B). Adding a path to
# one list without the other now reddens CI. The header's old "keep them in
# lockstep" plea became a test.
#
# NOT mirrored, deliberately: the guard's PROTECTED_BASENAMES (AGENTS.md,
# CLAUDE.md, .mcp.json, .npmrc, ... at ANY depth). Those are guard-only depth
# defence against a nested instruction/config file; CODEOWNERS matches known
# path locations, and the exact roots that matter (/AGENTS.md,
# /docs/state/registry.md) are already listed below. The lockstep test checks
# prefixes and exact paths only, for that reason.
#
# The invariant both controls enforce together: an implementing agent can
# never grant itself more pipeline power, and a human changing pipeline power
# always goes through an explicit, visible review by the operator.
#
# PRECEDENCE NOTE (factory-security review, LOW): GitHub reads CODEOWNERS
# from three possible locations, in this priority order — .github/,
# then the repo root, then docs/ — using only the FIRST one it finds.
# This file lives at the repo root, which is NOT top priority: a later,
# human-authored PR that adds a .github/CODEOWNERS would silently SHADOW
# every rule below with no error or warning from GitHub. If a
# .github/CODEOWNERS or docs/CODEOWNERS ever needs to exist, merge its
# rules into (or move this file to) whichever location must win, rather
# than letting two files quietly disagree about who owns what. The lockstep
# test also asserts .github/CODEOWNERS and a root .github-shadowing variant
# do not exist, so this hazard reddens CI if it is ever introduced.

# The factory's own workflow files — triggers, permissions, secrets,
# action pins, allowlists.
/.github/ @syamaner

# The privileged glue/publisher scripts (read-only-agent /
# privileged-publisher split, factory.md §13) — these decide what an
# agent's patch is allowed to do, so they must never be self-editable by
# that same patch.
/scripts/factory/ @syamaner

# All of .claude/ (subsumes the narrower spec-grounded-review rule this
# replaced): the sub-agent roster (.claude/agents/**, whose model pins the
# guard-protected enforcement test enforces, #160), every skill including
# spec-grounded-review (claude-code-review.yml's restore step re-applies it
# unconditionally from PR-head, same treatment as scripts/factory/), and
# .claude/settings*.json. The guard protects .claude/ wholesale; this
# mirrors it.
/.claude/ @syamaner

# .codex/ — the Codex CLI's own config/instruction surface, guard-protected
# wholesale for the same self-modification reason as .claude/.
/.codex/ @syamaner

# The factory-integrity enforcement tests (#160 — the writable-alarm class):
# the ONLY mechanical enforcement of pins/guards whose assets are already
# owned above. Protecting the asset while leaving the alarm writable let a
# factory patch gut the enforcement as a "test refactor". The subtree
# coincides exactly with that class; app-slice tests live in tests/ root,
# e2e/, and snowflake/tests/.
/tests/factory/ @syamaner

# This file itself — an agent (or anyone) editing CODEOWNERS to drop a
# protected path must go through the same review the paths it protects
# require, not bypass it by editing the control.
/CODEOWNERS @syamaner

# The guard's OTHER recognized CODEOWNERS location (findForbiddenPatchPaths'
# own PROTECTED_EXACT_PATHS lists both "CODEOWNERS" and "docs/CODEOWNERS"),
# and one of GitHub's three official locations — exactly why the guard
# defends it too. Nothing lives at this path today; owning it pre-emptively
# closes the gap where a human-directed PR could create or edit it without
# code-owner review (factory-security review, MEDIUM).
/docs/CODEOWNERS @syamaner

# The root agent instruction file — stack rules, gates, review routing that
# the guard reads. A conventional human-directed edit is expected; an agent
# patch is blocked outright by the guard.
/AGENTS.md @syamaner

# The live state registry — an exact protected path (D135): untrusted agent
# output cannot rewrite the source of truth, while trusted deterministic
# transition logic or a conventional same-PR amendment still may.
/docs/state/registry.md @syamaner
